\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The direction of the measure phase}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For their uncoupled oriented simplicial complex,
Catterall, Laiho and Unmuth-Yockey write the degree scaling
#{\omega_p\mapsto\ue^{\ui\vartheta(-1)^p}\omega_p}
and a measure phase #{\ue^{2\ui\vartheta\chi}}; see
\citet{§3, p. 4, (3.5)–(3.7)}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}.
Their skew-adjoint operator corresponds to
#{\extd -\extdag}, as explained in \ref{fdip-00S7}.}

\p{At rank one and #{u=\ue^{\ui\vartheta}},
\ref{fdip-00UF} gives
#{\mathcal I_V\circ\alpha_u^{-1}
 =\ue^{-2\ui\vartheta\chi}\mathcal I_V}.
The printed source phase has the opposite sign to this
explicit inverse pullback. Direct composition gives
#{\mathcal I_V\circ\alpha_u=u^{2\chi}\mathcal I_V}.
Both directions have the
same invariance condition #{u^{2\chi}=1}.
The finite identity uses cell parity and the chosen
coefficient convention; it asserts no chiral-fermion or
anomaly interpretation of the coupled construction.}
